&lt;Page 129&gt;
Strain
Characters get battered and bruised in more ways than physical injury. Strain represents stress, fatigue, desperation, worry, lack of focus, or being pushed to the limit. Since characters recover from strain quickly, you can have them suffer strain liberally to reflect these situations.
If a character hasn't slept in a while, they might suffer a certain amount of strain until they get a good night's rest. If the player wants to push their character to get just &quot;a little bit more&quot; out of an action, you might rule that they can do it if they're willing to suffer 2 strain.
Minor injuries and inconveniences such as twisting an ankle or falling out of a tree are better resolved with strain instead of wounds. Strain is also a great generalpurpose use for extra {A}

or {Th}

during skill checks, and can be described in a way that fits almost any situation.
Sources

Genesys Core Rules, p. 129

&lt;Page 23&gt;
Successes and Failures
When you roll a skill check, if the results include both Success symbols {S}

and Failure symbols {F}

, count up the total number of each type of symbol and then compare those two numbers. Each Failure symbol {F}

cancels one Success symbol {S}

. After you make all the needed cancellations due to Failure symbols {F}

, a pool of one or more Success symbols {S}

or one or more Failure symbols {F}

remains, or those symbols evenly cancel out.
During this step, it's important to note that a Triumph symbol {Tr}

contributes one Success {S}

to the pool, in addition to any special effects it may trigger. Likewise, a Despair symbol {D}

contributes one Failure {F}

to the pool, in addition to its special effects.
If all Successes {S}

and Failures {F}

in the pool are canceled out, or if any net Failures {F}

remain, the skill check fails. If at least one Success {S}

remains, the skill check succeeds. Remember, a dice pool must have at least one Success symbol {S}

remaining for a skill check to succeed.
Sources

Genesys Core Rules, p. 23

&lt;Page 129&gt;
Time and Timing
Having time pass is one way to help a game seem more real. Things happen even when the characters aren't
&lt;Page 130&gt;
directly involved. The passage of time also creates more context. Saying a character is injured but that they are ready to head back into action the day after a Medicine check creates a very different feeling than saying it takes days—or even weeks—to recover.
Player and character actions can have time-related consequences as well. If players delay making decisions, it could reflect uncertainty in their characters and make certain character actions take longer to accomplish. Poor planning, causing scenes in public, or a {D}

result on a check might give an enemy the time they need to slip away. Tampering with time by having an action take longer or resolve more quickly than expected is also a great use for {A}

and {Th}

.
To create a sense of tension, you can take time away from the characters. The PCs have only moments to prepare before meeting an important diplomat. They thought they had a month to recover the stolen painting, but their contact demands it by the end of the week. They are ambushed by an enemy. Limiting time can force the players' hands, trigger a sense of urgency, or create a spark of excitement if things start to drag.
Sources

Genesys Core Rules, pp. 129-130

&lt;Page 24&gt;
Triumph
A Triumph symbol {Tr}

has two effects. First, each Triumph symbol {Tr}

counts as one Success symbol {S}

. Second, a Triumph result indicates an unexpected boon or significantly beneficial effect related to the check your character just made. Many weapons and talents have side effects that can be triggered using a Triumph result. Otherwise, the scenario or your GM may present further options for using Triumph.
It's important to keep in mind that while a Triumph symbol {Tr}

does add a Success symbol {S}

to the pool, the presence of one or more Triumph symbols {Tr}

does not automatically mean that the check succeeded.
You can think of Triumph as an enhanced, more powerful version of Advantage. For example, a single Triumph symbol {Tr}

can trigger any weapon's special quality or inflict a Critical Injury, while without a Triumph, these effects require a certain number of Advantage symbols {A}

. Also, while Advantage may allow your character to heal strain, in the right situation, a Triumph {Tr}

may allow them to heal a wound.
Sources

Genesys Core Rules, p. 24

&lt;Page 24&gt;
Triumph and Despair
We've mentioned these two symbols before, but let's talk about them in more detail here. Triumph {Tr}

and Despair {D}

have more powerful effects on the game and work slightly differently than the other symbols. Unlike Success and Failure ({S}

and {F}

) or Advantage and Threat ({A}

and {Th}

), Triumph and Despair ({Tr}

and {D}

) do not completely cancel each other out. Instead, each indicates an especially positive or unfortunately dire side effect for your character's check.
Remember that it's possible for a skill check to result in both {Th}

and {D}

symbols. In this case, you should interpret both results separately. As with Threat, it's possible for a skill check to succeed but still impose a Despair effect, or fail but still trigger a Triumph effect. For more about the specific applications of Triumph and Despair, see pages 102, 103, and 121.
